<Base title="Research | Prashant Shah" bg="research">
  <header class="reveal">
    <h1 transition:name="page-title">The Research</h1>
    <p class="context">With Dr. Ali Al-Juboori at Ramapo College, 2025 to present.</p>
  </header>

  <div class="body">

    <section class="opening glass reveal" style="animation-delay: 0.1s">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>This started with a feeling everyone has had. You want to watch something, and you know the mood you're in, but the recommendations don't get it. You want a happy movie about war. Something calming about something high-stakes. The system gives you war movies or happy movies. It doesn't know how to hold both ideas at once.</p>
        <p>I built <a href="/work#emotionflix">EmotionFlix</a> to try to solve this with emotion detection. It worked, up to a point. But the deeper I went, the more I realized the problem wasn't in my system. It was in how retrieval itself works. Every retrieval system treats what you're looking for and how the content is described as the same signal. When those two things contradict each other, the system doesn't know what to do. It either respects your intent and ignores the content, or matches the content and ignores how you want to feel.</p>
        <p>Dr. Al-Juboori and I turned that gap into a research question.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="glass reveal" style="animation-delay: 0.18s">
      <h2>What we built</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>A framework that separates user intent from content semantics. The key is making emotions first-class entities in a knowledge graph, not metadata tags on existing nodes, but their own nodes with their own relationships.</p>
        <p>The system maintains a heterogeneous knowledge graph over MovieLens 25M with User, Movie, and Emotion nodes. Emotions connect to movies through weighted EVOKES edges. A Graph Transformer encoder aligns this structure to SentenceBERT space, and hybrid scoring applies a tunable affective displacement penalty.</p>
        <p>In plain terms: the graph can represent both "what this movie is about" and "how this movie makes people feel" as separate, traversable relationships. When those two signals conflict, the system can reason about both instead of choosing one.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="glass reveal" style="animation-delay: 0.25s">
      <h2>What we found</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>We tested 1,600 synthetic queries across aligned and conflicting conditions. In conflict scenarios, the framework achieved a 0.037 reduction in normalized affective displacement error compared to baseline retrieval.</p>
        <p>The more interesting finding: edge-removal causal interventions confirmed that emotion edges are causally necessary for the improvement. Remove them, and performance degrades to baseline. The graph structure is doing real work.</p>
        <p>Movies are the testbed. The architecture applies anywhere user affect and content semantics can diverge: music, therapeutic content, educational material. The framework is designed to be domain-agnostic.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="links reveal" style="animation-delay: 0.3s">
      <a href="https://github.com/Prashant002-1/knowledge-graph-retrieval-with-affective-grounding" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">See the code &rarr;</a>
    </div>

  </div>
</Base>

